const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How are aluminium aerosol cans manufactured?",
    a: "Aluminium aerosol cans are produced by impact extrusion, in which a die forces a punched aluminium slug to flow into a seamless two-piece body and shoulder in a single stroke. The formed cup is then trimmed, washed, internally lacquer-coated and cured, necked to accept a standard valve, and dimensionally checked before printing and dispatch."
  },
  {
    q: "How are tinplate aerosol cans manufactured?",
    a: "Tinplate aerosol cans are formed from electrolytically tin-plated mild steel sheet using either a two-piece drawn-and-ironed process or a three-piece construction with a rolled, seam-welded body and separately fitted top and bottom ends. Cans are then internally coated, printed, and inspected for seam integrity before shipment to fillers."
  },
  {
    q: "What is the difference between aluminium and tinplate aerosol cans?",
    a: "Aluminium cans are formed by impact extrusion into a seamless two-piece body, offering lower weight and good dent resistance, while tinplate cans are formed from sheet steel into two- or three-piece constructions with different strength characteristics depending on format. Material choice affects weight, corrosion behavior, cost, and the internal coating strategy required for product compatibility."
  },
  {
    q: "Why do aerosol cans need internal lacquer coatings?",
    a: "Internal lacquer coatings — including epoxy, phenolic, or polyester chemistries — isolate the metal substrate from the product formulation to prevent corrosion and metal-product interaction. Coating adhesion depends on substrate cleanliness and cure temperature, and coating failure is a common root cause of can corrosion and product contamination."
  },
  {
    q: "What safety tests do aerosol cans undergo before filling?",
    a: "Aerosol containers are tested for dimensional accuracy, seam integrity, and pressure performance, including normal-temperature and high-temperature pressure tests, burst testing, and leak detection by water bath or pressure decay. In the United States, disposable aerosol containers are commonly built to DOT 2P or 2Q specifications, which define construction materials and test pressure ranges."
  },
  {
    q: "What causes crimp leakage in aerosol can manufacturing?",
    a: "Crimp leakage commonly results from mismatched valve flange dimensions, worn crimp tooling, or incorrect crimp height and roll pressure during the filling line's valve-attachment step. Verifying flange dimensions against crimp tooling specifications and maintaining tooling condition are standard corrective measures."
  }
];

