<!-- INTRODUCTION -->
<section class="section" id="introduction">
  <h2>Introduction</h2>
  <p>Aerosol cans are engineered pressure vessels designed to contain product, propellant and a dispensing
                valve while withstanding internal pressure, handling and transport. The two dominant metal substrates in
                the aerosol industry are impact-extruded aluminium (typically two-piece) and tinplate steel (two- or
                three-piece constructions). Understanding how each is made, finished and tested is essential for product
                compatibility, safety and reliable filling-line operation. See our guide to
                <a href="/how-aerosol-cans-work/">how aerosol cans work</a> for how the finished can interacts with the
                valve and propellant once filled.</p>
            <figure>
                
                <figcaption>Overview of aluminium and tinplate aerosol can structures</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="aluminium-vs-tinplate">
</section>

<!-- ALUMINIUM VS TINPLATE — QUICK ORIENTATION PARAGRAPH (3–4 LINES) -->
<section class="section" id="aluminium-vs-tinplate-quick-orientation-paragraph-34-lines">
  <h2>Aluminium vs Tinplate: Quick Comparison</h2>
  <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Aspect</th>
                        <th>Aluminium</th>
                        <th>Tinplate</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Raw Material</td>
                        <td>Aluminium alloys</td>
                        <td>Electrolytically-tin-plated mild steel sheet</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Forming Method</td>
                        <td>Impact extrusion from a slug into a seamless two-piece body</td>
                        <td>Sheet forming, welded/three-piece or drawn-and-ironed two-piece bodies</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Mechanical Properties</td>
                        <td>Light weight, good dent resistance</td>
                        <td>Different strength characteristics based on format</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Surface Treatment</td>
                        <td>Specific corrosion protection and barrier coatings</td>
                        <td>Robust internal coatings for corrosion control</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>Aluminium and tinplate differ in raw material, forming method, mechanical properties and surface
                treatment requirements. Aluminium is most commonly formed by impact extrusion from a slug into a
                seamless two-piece body; tinplate cans are formed from sheet steel and may be manufactured as
                welded/three-piece or drawn-and-ironed two-piece bodies. These differences drive decisions on tooling
                cost, corrosion protection, barrier coatings and filling-line handling.</p>

            <h3>Key takeaway: aluminium → impact extrusion, seamless two-piece; tinplate → sheet forming, two- or
                three-piece options.</h3>
            <h3>Key takeaway: material choice affects weight, dent resistance, corrosion control and cost.</h3>
        </section>

        <section id="materials">
</section>

<!-- MATERIALS AND CORE REQUIREMENTS -->
<section class="section" id="materials-and-core-requirements">
  <h2>Materials and core requirements</h2>
  <p>Metal aerosol cans must meet mechanical strength and chemical-compatibility requirements: yield/ductility
                for drawing/necking, consistent wall thickness, and substrate cleanliness for coatings to adhere.
                Internal coatings (lacquers/epoxy/polymer layers) are applied to prevent metal–product interactions and
                corrosion, and external inks/varnishes provide decoration and abrasion resistance.</p>

            <p>Typical aluminium feedstock: punched slugs of aluminium alloys selected for impact extrusion.</p>
            <p>Typical tinplate feedstock: electrolytically-tin-plated mild steel sheet for forming and seaming.</p>
            <p>Coatings: internal lacquers (epoxy/phenolic/polyester chemistries) and external primers/inks are standard
                to prevent corrosion and migration. See our analysis of
                <a href="/aerosol-can-corrosion-1/">aerosol can corrosion causes and control</a> for the electrochemical
                mechanisms these coatings are designed to prevent.</p>
